The new Forsaken Paladin is the first new hero for Warcraft III multiplayer in 20 years. He is available for free via the Tavern in Classic, Reforged, and Definitive Edition modes.

The Forsaken Paladin's four abilities:

1. Righteous Fury
The Paladin makes a swift dash forward:
- deals damage to nearby enemies;
- slows down the enemies hit;
- allows for quickly initiating combat, catching a fleeing hero, or diving into the enemy backline.
It offers a combination of mobility, damage, and crowd control.

2. Consecration
An instant-cast area-of-effect ability centered on the Paladin:
- heals friendly living units;
- deals damage to enemies;
- allied Undead units take no damage, but also receive no healing or other positive effects.
This makes the ability particularly interesting when playing the Paladin with Human, Orc, or Night Elf factions. In an Undead army, it functions primarily as an area-of-effect damage spell.

3. Sacred Aura
Passively increases magic resistance for nearby allied units.
It helps to:
- withstand magic damage;
- defend against mass spells;
- reduce the effectiveness of magic attacks and hero "nukes";
- keep the army alive longer during large-scale battles.
It is especially useful against armies that rely heavily on magic.

4. Cleansing Fire
The hero's most powerful mass-effect ability. The Paladin summons the Light to affect the area around him:
- Heals nearby allies; - empowers allied troops;
- stuns enemies;
- dispels magical effects;
- deals massive damage to summoned creatures.

It serves simultaneously as a heal, a crowd-control tool, and a mass Dispel. It is particularly effective against summons: wolves, water elementals, skeletons, lava spawn, and other summoned units.

A brief overview of the hero's role: The Forsaken Paladin appears to be a versatile support and initiation hero, featuring a dash, a slow, mass healing, magic protection, a stun, and Dispel. A key characteristic is his utility for almost any race, though Consecration does not heal allied Undead.

